From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 23:17:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/34] wasm: seed new account databases from a pre-migrated
 template

Creating an account replays every migration past the baseline schema,
each in its own transaction, so each one commits WAL frames through
separate slow OPFS sync-access-handle writes: ~1.7s per account, and
the cost is the write count, not the CPU (the same migrations run in
~52ms against the in-memory VFS).

Pre-fill a NEW account's database file from an already-migrated
template instead, so opening it is one bulk write and the migration
run finds dbversion current. Existing databases are never touched, so
this is safe on every open, and the template is optional: none
installed, or one built before a migration landed, and the account
just migrates the (remaining) way as before.
---
 src/sql.rs | 41 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/sql.rs b/src/sql.rs
index 406d7c5..6ffa5f1 100644
--- a/src/sql.rs
+++ b/src/sql.rs
@@ -90,6 +90,33 @@ pub async fn check_passphrase(&self, passphrase: String) -> Result<bool> {
         // Hold the lock to prevent other thread from opening the database.
         let _lock = self.pool.write().await;
 
+        // wasm: a brand-new account otherwise replays every migration past the
+        // baseline schema, each in its own transaction and so its own batch of
+        // OPFS writes — ~1.7s per account, dominated by write count, not CPU.
+        // Pre-filling the file from an already-migrated database collapses that
+        // to one bulk write; the migration run then finds `dbversion` current
+        // and does nothing. A template built before a migration landed just
+        // replays the ones added since, so it stays correct, only slower.
+        //
+        // Here rather than in `try_open`: this is the first thing that ever
+        // touches the file — `Connection::open` below CREATES it — and it is
+        // also where the passphrase is known. Existing databases are never
+        // touched, so every later open is unaffected. It also puts the seed
+        // right before the probe that can undo it, see below.
+        //
+        // One semantic change to know about when writing a migration: a seeded
+        // database already has a `config` table, so `migrations::run` sees
+        // `exists_before_update == true` for what is in fact a brand-new
+        // account. No current migration branches on that (they all sit behind
+        // `dbversion <` gates a matched template is already past), but one that
+        // did would hand new accounts the upgrade path.
+        #[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
+        let seeded = passphrase.is_empty()
+            && self
+                .dbfile
+                .to_str()
+                .is_some_and(tokio::fs::seed_db_from_template);
+
         // Test that the key is correct using a single connection.
         let connection = Connection::open(&self.dbfile)?;
         if !passphrase.is_empty() {
@@ -101,6 +128,20 @@ pub async fn check_passphrase(&self, passphrase: String) -> Result<bool> {
             .query_row("SELECT count(*) FROM sqlite_master", [], |_row| Ok(()))
             .is_ok();
 
+        // A template that imported cleanly can still be unreadable — the VFS
+        // only checks the file header, not the schema. Without this, a new
+        // account would be created around a database that never opens and
+        // never heals (it exists, so it is never seeded again), which is a
+        // worse failure than the slow path this whole thing avoids. Undo the
+        // seed and let sqlite create a real empty database instead, and drop
+        // the template so no other account pays for it either.
+        #[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
+        if seeded && !key_is_correct {
+            drop(connection);
+            tokio::fs::discard_db_template(self.dbfile.to_str().unwrap_or_default());
+            return Ok(true);
+        }
+
         Ok(key_is_correct)
     }
 
